<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#lord-protector">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lord Protector</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lords Protector, Lord Protector of the Commonwealth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lord Protector is a title used for a royal prince or noble who protected and defended the realm and governed for a monarch unable to do so, and later for the head of state of the Commonwealth. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the dukes of Bedford and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucester">Gloucester</a> were jointly Lords Protector for Henry VI from 1422, and Richard, Duke of Gloucester held the title in 1483 before taking the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a>. Under the Instrument of Government of 1653 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> became Lord Protector of the Commonwealth of England, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and Ireland for life; his son Richard succeeded him in September 1658 and resigned in May 1659. Since the Restoration the title has not been used in these ways.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its General Council debated the constitution at Putney in 1647, and in 1653 soldiers expelled the Rump Parliament and army officers drafted the Instrument of Government that made Oliver Cromwell <b>Lord Protector</b>.</blockquote><a href="/government/military-junta">Military junta · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…North America elected assemblies sat beside governors, appointed by the Crown or the proprietors in most colonies but chosen by the colonists themselves in a few, such as Connecticut and Rhode Island, and in England the Instrument of Government of 1653 had set a <b>Lord Protector</b> beside Parliament.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Montesquieu and Rousseau thought republics workable only in small states, and the Commonwealth of England, which ended in Oliver Cromwell’s personal rule as <b>Lord Protector</b>, persuaded many that republics led to anarchy or tyranny.</blockquote><a href="/government/republic">Republic · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>The act paved the way for his rule as <b>Lord Protector</b> later that year.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 1653, after the failure of Barebone's Parliament, army officers drew up the Instrument of Government, Britain's first written constitution, which made Oliver Cromwell <b>Lord Protector</b> of the Commonwealth of England, Scotland and Ireland, with a Council of State and Parliament.</blockquote><a href="/state/the-protectorate">The Protectorate</a></li></ul></section>
